.TH tuzip n 0.2 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tuzip
.PP
Small ZIP helper written in pure Tcl.
.SS Package
.CS
package require tclutils::tuzip
.CE
.SS Commands
.CS
::tclutils::tuzip::entries archive.zip
::tclutils::tuzip::names archive.zip
::tclutils::tuzip::readMember archive.zip member/name.txt
::tclutils::tuzip::extract archive.zip member/name.txt out.txt
::tclutils::tuzip::create archive.zip $files -base $directory -compress 1
.CE
.SS Notes
.PP
\fBtuzip\fR currently supports stored entries and deflated entries. This is enough for common ZIP files and for inspecting ODF files such as \fB.odt\fR, \fB.ods\fR, \fB.odg\fR and \fB.odp\fR, because those files are ZIP containers.
.PP
Binary files are always opened in binary mode. \fBcreate\fR, \fBcreateMembers\fR and \fBextract\fR write through a temporary file in the target folder and rename it: an error while writing leaves the old file (up to 2026\-10\-30 the target was opened first and stayed empty). \fBtudocx\fR, \fBtuxlsx\fR and \fBtuodf\fR write through \fBcreateMembers\fR and get the same.
.PP
Member names (0.2): written as UTF\-8. A name with non\-ASCII characters gets the UTF\-8 flag (general purpose bit 11), and the central directory says "made on Unix", so Info\-ZIP \fBunzip\fR, Python and Windows read \fBJörg.txt\fR as \fBJörg.txt\fR. Up to 0.1 names were written as Latin\-1 without the flag (other programs saw \fBJ÷rg.txt\fR, a \fB€\fR became \fB?\fR).
.PP
\fBentries\fR, \fBnames\fR, \fBreadMember\fR and \fBextract\fR take \fB\-encoding\fR:
.TS
l l.
value	names without the UTF\-8 flag
\fBauto\fR (default)	UTF\-8 when the bytes are valid UTF\-8, else CP437 (the ZIP default)
any Tcl encoding	that encoding, e.g. \fBiso8859\-1\fR for archives written by tuzip 0.1
.TE
.PP
Names with the flag are always UTF\-8. An unknown encoding is an error \fB{TCLUTILS TUZIP ENCODING}\fR.
.SS "Additional exported commands"
.PP
Documented for completeness (same module, also covered by the test suite):
.CS
tuzip::createMembers zipfile members args      ;# create a ZIP from in-memory members with per-entry compression and explicit order. `members` is a list of dicts, each with keys: name (archive path, required), content (raw bytes, required), method (stored|deflate, default deflate). The member order is preserved -- e.g. an ODF container can put `mimetype` first as stored and compress the XML parts in a single call. Option -mtime EPOCH sets a fixed timestamp for all entries (default: now)
.CE
